---
title: The case against automatic filing
order: 3
summary: What we let the system suggest, and what we insist a lawyer decides.
minutes: 4
topic: Product decisions
draft: false
---

The obvious pitch for an AI-era DMS is that the AI files everything for you. We built that version first, and we tested it on a mock corpus with lawyers who had agreed to be honest with us.

It filed most documents correctly. The ones it got wrong were the problem. A mis-filed document is worse than an unfiled one, because an unfiled document is at least visibly missing. A mis-filed one looks handled. Within a week the lawyers in the test had stopped trusting the filing they had not checked, which meant they were checking all of it, which meant the automation was saving nobody anything.

### What we kept

The system suggests. The lawyer decides. Lucio Capsule sits on screen while you work in Word or Outlook. Drop a document on it, or version up from it, and it proposes the matter, the folder and the label. Confirm is one click. Changing the suggestion is two. Holding it for later is also one.

{% aside label="Where the suggestion comes from" %}
Mostly from outside the document. The subject line of the email it was attached to. Who sent it. The meeting it was circulated before. "Execution copy attached, per the call" is a much stronger signal that this is the signing version than anything in the text of the agreement itself. Capsule shows that reason next to the suggestion so the lawyer can see why it is being proposed.
{% /aside %}

### Why this is also the safer position

Nothing in Lucio DMS is labelled without a person. That is an operating principle, and it is also the position that clients, regulators and insurers already understand. When someone asks who decided this document was the executed version, the answer is a name and a time.

We think this is what filing should feel like in practice. Not a chore that competes with billable work, and not a black box that does it behind your back. A two-second confirmation that happens at the moment you were already saving the file.
